UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 15, Lesson 2: Unit review and showcase', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A healthy food showcase brings together the five food groups, balanced plates, everyday and sometimes foods, water and food safety. Choose a clear way to present, such as a poster, a recipe card or a short talk, and use real examples with reasons. Be respectful of different foods, cultures, budgets and allergies when talking about meals.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWrite two sentences for a showcase saying why a mixed lunch is healthier than a lunch of chips and soft drink. Name food groups in your reasons.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSample: ''A mixed lunch with a sandwich, salad, fruit and water uses four food groups, which give different nutrients. Chips and soft drink mostly give salt, fat and sugar and no variety.'' The reasons name groups and nutrients.\n\nWATCH FOR\nBelieving a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love. A healthy diet is about balance and variety, with enjoyment included.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nMake a showcase about healthy eating for a younger child. Include the five groups, a balanced plate, one sometimes food rule, why water matters and two food safety steps. Present it to a family member and collect two stars and a wish.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"A healthy food showcase brings together the five food groups, balanced plates, everyday and sometimes foods, water and food safety. Choose a clear way to present, such as a poster, a recipe card or a short talk, and use real examples with reasons. Be respectful of different foods, cultures, budgets and allergies when talking about meals.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Practise and apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-IHW-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Make a showcase about healthy eating for a younger child. Include the five groups, a balanced plate, one sometimes food rule, why water matters and two food safety steps. Present it to a family member and collect two sta Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original demonstration: Example: Leo''s recipe card ''Rainbow wraps''. Food groups: grain (wrap), protein (chicken), vegetables (carrot, lettuce, capsicum), milk (cheese). Safety: wash hands and use separate boards. Drink: water. Sometimes food: a small cookie on Fridays. He gives a reason for each choice.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Believing a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love. A healthy diet is about balance and variety, with enjoyment included. Correction: Sample: ''A mixed lunch with a sandwich, salad, fruit and water uses four food groups, which give different nutrients. Chips and soft drink mostly give salt, fat and sugar and no variety.'' The reasons name groups and nutrients.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Believing a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love. A healthy diet is about balance and variety, with enjoyment included. Keep sample answers out of the child role.","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A healthy food showcase brings together the five food groups, balanced plates, everyday and sometimes foods, water and food safety. Choose a clear way to present, such as a poster, a recipe card or a short talk, and use real examples with reasons. Be respectful of different foods, cultures, budgets and allergies when talking about meals. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["A healthy food showcase brings together the five food groups, balanced plates, everyday and sometimes foods, water and food safety. Choose a clear way to present, such as a poster, a recipe card or a short talk, and use real examples with reasons. Be respectful of different foods, cultures, budgets and allergies when talking about meals.","Believing a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love. A healthy diet is about balance and variety, with enjoyment included.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Copy the example and call that independent work."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Understand and demonstrate\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.","Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking.","Copy the example and call that independent work.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Show the reasoning\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.","Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Believing a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love. A healthy diet is about balance and variety, with enjoyment included.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter.","Leave someone out because the game felt easier that way."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Test a misconception\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation.","Leave someone out because the game felt easier that way.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Transfer to a new situation\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Sample: ''A mixed lunch with a sandwich, salad, fruit and water uses four food groups, which give different nutrients. Chips and soft drink mostly give salt, fat and sugar and no variety.'' The reasons name groups and nutrients. Useful feedback uses the original cues, rules and reasons.","options":["Sample: ''A mixed lunch with a sandwich, salad, fruit and water uses four food groups, which give different nutrients. Chips and soft drink mostly give salt, fat and sugar and no variety.'' The reasons name groups and nutrients.","Believing a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love. A healthy diet is about balance and variety, with enjoyment included.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Write two sentences for a showcase saying why a mixed lunch is healthier than a \", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\". Useful feedback uses the original cues, rules and reasons.","options":["The most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"List the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does \", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Sample: ''A mixed lunch with a sandwich, salad, fruit and water uses four food groups, which give different nutrients. Chips and soft drink mostly give salt, fat and sugar and no variety.'' The reasons name groups and nutrients. Useful feedback uses the original cues, rules and reasons.","options":["Sample: ''A mixed lunch with a sandwich, salad, fruit and water uses four food groups, which give different nutrients. Chips and soft drink mostly give salt, fat and sugar and no variety.'' The reasons name groups and nutrients.","Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Believing a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love. A healthy diet is about balance and variety, with enjoyment included.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Describe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people. Useful feedback uses the original cues, rules and reasons.","options":["The new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Describe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a sh\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence, not repetition of the worked model, demonstrates the skill.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced relevant evidence with accurate reasoning and named cues, rules or choices.","question":"Describe or practise a fresh situation that uses the central skill. Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included.","sample_answer":"Original demonstration: Example: Leo''s recipe card ''Rainbow wraps''. Food groups: grain (wrap), protein (chicken), vegetables (carrot, lettuce, capsicum), milk (cheese). Safety: wash hands and use separate boards. Drink: water. Sometimes food: a small cookie on Fridays. He gives a reason for each choice.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A correction needs to change the choice or strategy, not just make the answer longer.","marking_guide":"Require an actual misconception plus a corrected choice and a reason linked to safety, health or respect.","question":"Name one likely misconception or unsafe choice for this topic, correct it and say why the correction is safer, healthier or more respectful.","sample_answer":"Common error: Believing a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love. A healthy diet is about balance and variety, with enjoyment included. Correction: Sample: ''A mixed lunch with a sandwich, salad, fruit and water uses four food groups, which give different nutrients. Chips and soft drink mostly give salt, fat and sugar and no variety.'' The reasons name groups and nutrients.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w15-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise a showcase.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Healthy, Safe and Active Lifestyles","success_criteria":["I can use a showcase with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify a balanced meal and explain why it matters.","I can practise reasons independently on a new prompt.","I can notice respect for differences needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A healthy food showcase brings together the five food groups, balanced plates, everyday and sometimes foods, water and food safety. Choose a clear way to present, such as a poster, a recipe card or a short talk, and use real examples with reasons. Be respectful of different foods, cultures, budgets and allergies when talking about meals. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["A healthy food showcase brings together the five food groups, balanced plates, everyday and sometimes foods, water and food safety. Choose a clear way to present, such as a poster, a recipe card or a short talk, and use real examples with reasons. Be respectful of different foods, cultures, budgets and allergies when talking about meals.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Leo''s recipe card ''Rainbow wraps''. Food groups: grain (wrap), protein (chicken), vegetables (carrot, lettuce, capsicum), milk (cheese). Safety: wash hands and use separate boards. Drink: water. Sometimes food: a small cookie on Fridays. He gives a reason for each choice.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite two sentences for a showcase saying why a mixed lunch is healthier than a lunch of chips and soft drink. Name food groups in your reasons.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSample: ''A mixed lunch with a sandwich, salad, fruit and water uses four food groups, which give different nutrients. Chips and soft drink mostly give salt, fat and sugar and no variety.'' The reasons name groups and nutrients.","explain":"A healthy food showcase brings together the five food groups, balanced plates, everyday and sometimes foods, water and food safety. Choose a clear way to present, such as a poster, a recipe card or a short talk, and use real examples with reasons. Be respectful of different foods, cultures, budgets and allergies when talking about meals.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Believing a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love. A healthy diet is about balance and variety, with enjoyment included. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"A balanced wrap.","caption":"Four groups in one meal.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Wrap</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">grain, protein, vegetables, milk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Drink</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">water</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: Leo''s recipe card ''Rainbow wraps''. Food groups: grain (wrap), protein (chicken), vegetables (carrot, lettuce, c Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: Leo''s recipe card ''Rainbow wraps''. Food groups: grain (wrap), protein (chicken), vegetables (carrot, lettuce, c Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Leo''s recipe card ''Rainbow wraps''. Food groups: grain (wrap), protein (chicken), vegetables (carrot, lettuce, capsicum), milk (cheese). Safety: wash hands and use separate boards. Drink: water. Sometimes food: a small cookie on Fridays. He gives a reason for each choice. Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"Respect for food differences.","caption":"Respect every table.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Respect</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">different foods and budgets</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Allergy</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">plan for safe foods</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBelieving a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love. A healthy diet is about balance and variety, with enjoyment included. Here is a more helpful example: Example: Leo''s recipe card ''Rainbow wraps''. Food groups: grain (wrap), protein (chicken), vegetables (carrot, lettuce, capsicum), milk (cheese). Safety: wash hands and use separate boards. Drink: water. Sometimes food: a small cookie on Fridays. He gives a reason for each choice.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSample: ''A mixed lunch with a sandwich, salad, fruit and water uses four food groups, which give different nutrients. Chips and soft drink mostly give salt, fat and sugar and no variety.'' The reasons name groups and nutrients.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Believing a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love. A healthy diet is about balance and variety, with enjoyment included. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA healthy food showcase brings together the five food groups, balanced plates, everyday and sometimes foods, water and food safety. Choose a clear way to present, such as a poster, a recipe card or a short talk, and use real examples with reasons. Be respectful of different foods, cultures, budgets and allergies when talking about meals. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 15, Lesson 2: Unit review and showcase","unit_number":3,"unit_title":"Healthy food and nutrition","week":15,"week_in_unit":5,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: Leo''s recipe card ''Rainbow wraps''. Food groups: grain (wrap), protein (chicken), vegetables (carrot, lettuce, capsicum), milk (cheese). Safety: wash hands and use separate boards. Drink: water. Sometimes food: a small cookie on Fridays. He gives a reason for each choice.\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"A balanced wrap.","caption":"Four groups in one meal.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><r